    {
      "id": "oM1EiRBSg0uf5QGzpOGSH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 68.20794229554554,
      "y": 2650.5380056536414,
      "width": 990,
      "height": 2525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 438833640,
      "version": 5,
      "versionNonce": 1388043752,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785949488031,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Why not render everything once?\n\n* 100K DOM/SVG nodes increase memory, style, layout, paint, and React reconciliation cost.\n* Canvas/WebGL still wastes frame time drawing offscreen objects.\n* Use spatial index + viewport culling.\n* Target: O(log N + K) where K = visible objects.\n\n2. What if an offscreen layer is selected?\n\n* Selection is stored by stable object ID, not mounted row.\n* Resolve object bounds.\n* Reveal it on canvas.\n* Expand ancestors if needed.\n* Scroll virtual tree to its row.\n* Keep selection, visibility, and focus as separate states.\n\n3. How do variable row heights work?\n\n* Start with estimated heights.\n* Measure mounted rows using ResizeObserver.\n* Store heights in a cache.\n* Use prefix sums/Fenwick tree for offsets.\n* Binary-search scrollTop → row index.\n* Preserve a scroll anchor to avoid jumping.\n\n4. How do you expand a 50K-child group?\n\n* Never create 50K React elements.\n* Mark expanded immediately.\n* Render only the visible range.\n* Flatten in worker/chunks.\n* Lazy-load child metadata.\n* Cancel stale expansion work if collapsed.\n\n5. How is canvas content accessible?\n\n* Canvas renders pixels; DOM exposes semantics.\n* Provide accessible layer tree.\n* Add keyboard move/resize/reorder controls.\n* Expose selected-object properties.\n* Use aria-live for important actions.\n* Support focus, zoom, high contrast, and reduced motion.\n\n6. What changes for collaboration?\n\n* Represent committed edits as operations.\n* Apply local edits optimistically.\n* Server orders/validates operations.\n* Keep cursor/presence separate from durable history.\n* Reconcile pending local and remote operations.\n* Undo only the local user’s eligible operations.\n\n7. How do you avoid stale worker results?\n\n* Add request ID and document/viewport revision.\n* Apply result only if revisions still match.\n* Use latest-request-wins semantics.\n* Send incremental deltas instead of full snapshots.\n* Make long worker jobs cancellable.\n\n8. How do you recover after WebGL context loss?\n\n* Pause rendering on webglcontextlost.\n* Keep document state outside GPU memory.\n* Recreate shaders, buffers, textures, and framebuffers.\n* Restore visible resources first.\n* Lazily rebuild offscreen caches.\n* Fall back to Canvas/reduced effects if recovery fails.\n\n9. How do you bound memory?\n\n* Normalize document state.\n* Virtualize DOM rows.\n* Use byte-bounded LRU caches.\n* Load zoom-appropriate image variants.\n* Evict offscreen textures.\n* Compact undo history.\n* Use transferable worker data.\n* Every cache needs a budget and eviction policy.\n\n10. When do you choose Canvas vs WebGL?\n\nCanvas\n\n* Simpler implementation\n* Better native text support\n* Moderate visible object count\n* Basic shapes/images\n* Meets frame budget\n\nWebGL\n\n* Dense visible scenes\n* Heavy compositing/effects\n* GPU batching and instancing\n* Canvas becomes CPU bottleneck\n\nDecision\n\n* Start with Canvas.\n* Move to WebGL only when profiling justifies the complexity.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Why not render everything once?\n\n* 100K DOM/SVG nodes increase memory, style, layout, paint, and React reconciliation cost.\n* Canvas/WebGL still wastes frame time drawing offscreen objects.\n* Use spatial index + viewport culling.\n* Target: O(log N + K) where K = visible objects.\n\n2. What if an offscreen layer is selected?\n\n* Selection is stored by stable object ID, not mounted row.\n* Resolve object bounds.\n* Reveal it on canvas.\n* Expand ancestors if needed.\n* Scroll virtual tree to its row.\n* Keep selection, visibility, and focus as separate states.\n\n3. How do variable row heights work?\n\n* Start with estimated heights.\n* Measure mounted rows using ResizeObserver.\n* Store heights in a cache.\n* Use prefix sums/Fenwick tree for offsets.\n* Binary-search scrollTop → row index.\n* Preserve a scroll anchor to avoid jumping.\n\n4. How do you expand a 50K-child group?\n\n* Never create 50K React elements.\n* Mark expanded immediately.\n* Render only the visible range.\n* Flatten in worker/chunks.\n* Lazy-load child metadata.\n* Cancel stale expansion work if collapsed.\n\n5. How is canvas content accessible?\n\n* Canvas renders pixels; DOM exposes semantics.\n* Provide accessible layer tree.\n* Add keyboard move/resize/reorder controls.\n* Expose selected-object properties.\n* Use aria-live for important actions.\n* Support focus, zoom, high contrast, and reduced motion.\n\n6. What changes for collaboration?\n\n* Represent committed edits as operations.\n* Apply local edits optimistically.\n* Server orders/validates operations.\n* Keep cursor/presence separate from durable history.\n* Reconcile pending local and remote operations.\n* Undo only the local user’s eligible operations.\n\n7. How do you avoid stale worker results?\n\n* Add request ID and document/viewport revision.\n* Apply result only if revisions still match.\n* Use latest-request-wins semantics.\n* Send incremental deltas instead of full snapshots.\n* Make long worker jobs cancellable.\n\n8. How do you recover after WebGL context loss?\n\n* Pause rendering on webglcontextlost.\n* Keep document state outside GPU memory.\n* Recreate shaders, buffers, textures, and framebuffers.\n* Restore visible resources first.\n* Lazily rebuild offscreen caches.\n* Fall back to Canvas/reduced effects if recovery fails.\n\n9. How do you bound memory?\n\n* Normalize document state.\n* Virtualize DOM rows.\n* Use byte-bounded LRU caches.\n* Load zoom-appropriate image variants.\n* Evict offscreen textures.\n* Compact undo history.\n* Use transferable worker data.\n* Every cache needs a budget and eviction policy.\n\n10. When do you choose Canvas vs WebGL?\n\nCanvas\n\n* Simpler implementation\n* Better native text support\n* Moderate visible object count\n* Basic shapes/images\n* Meets frame budget\n\nWebGL\n\n* Dense visible scenes\n* Heavy compositing/effects\n* GPU batching and instancing\n* Canvas becomes CPU bottleneck\n\nDecision\n\n* Start with Canvas.\n* Move to WebGL only when profiling justifies the complexity.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
